==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹæbəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-rabble.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æbəl}}

===Etymology 1===
First attested since 1300s, from {{inh|en|enm|rablen||to ramble; rave; speak in a confused manner}}, cognate with {{cog|dum|rabbelen||to talk; chatter; trifle}}, {{cog|nds|rabbeln}}, {{m|nds|robbeln||to chatter; prattle}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ravel}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|rabbl|ing}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[speak]] in a [[confused]] manner; talk [[incoherently]]; [[utter]] [[nonsense]]
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To speak confusedly or incoherently; [[gabble]] or [[chatter]] out

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|rabel}}, probably from the verb (see above).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[bewildered]] or [[meaningless]] string of words.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[pack]] of animals; or any confused collection of things.
# A [[mob]]; a [[disorderly]] crowd. {{defdate|from late 14<sup>th</sup> c.}} 
# {{lb|en|contemptuous|derogatory}} The mass of [[common]] people; the lowest [[class]] of [[populace]]. {{defdate|from 1550s}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|en|plebs}}
* {{l|en|riffraff}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|rabble rouser}}
* {{l|en|rabblesome}}

